# Parsing without side effects

Parsing answers what a project says before any host decides what to do with it.
Literate keeps this stage free of filesystem writes, editor APIs, browser launches,
and external processes. Explicit source documents go in; a parsed project and
source-position diagnostics come out.

This boundary matters because several consumers need the same interpretation:
normal generation, inspection, screen publication, print publication, fragment
navigation, and generated-language projection.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Snapshot[Captured project snapshot] --> Parse[Parse once]
    Parse --> Model[Parsed project]
    Model --> Inspect[Inspection]
    Model --> Generate[Generation]
    Model --> Screen[Screen rendering]
    Model --> Print[Print publication]
    Model --> Project[Language projection]
```

The arrows represent consumers of one model, not independent parsers. A consumer
may apply stricter policy, but it should not quietly reinterpret fragment syntax.

## Markdown remains a document language

A `.literate` file is ordinary Markdown with a narrow fragment convention added to
code-fence headers. Markdown-it determines block structure, nested containers,
inline code, links, tables, and fence boundaries. Literate examines the resulting
tokens rather than maintaining a competing Markdown parser.

This has two consequences. First, fragment declarations obey real Markdown fence
rules, including nesting and unclosed fences. Second, ordinary code blocks and
inline examples remain document content unless their headers deliberately declare
a fragment.

The parser retains the actual token stream for each accepted document. Publication
can then preserve lists, quotations, headings, and other containers without trying
to reconstruct them from rendered HTML.

## A parsed project serves several levels of detail

The parsed result contains related but distinct views:

- **Diagnostics** identify invalid source declarations and locations.
- **Inspection** lists documents, templates, definitions, appends, and references.
- **Publication data** records ordered contributions and stable logical targets.
- **Document blocks** preserve the relationship between Markdown and contribution
  fences.

A diagnostic does not make every observation useless. Inspection may still show
where a malformed or duplicate declaration was found. Publication and generation,
however, must respect the diagnostics that gate their own outputs.

## Source locations survive normalization

Markdown normalizes line endings and fence contents for convenient processing.
Editor diagnostics still need positions in the original source. Literate therefore
retains source paths, zero-based lines, and UTF-16 columns alongside normalized
content.

UTF-16 is not chosen as a measure of human-visible characters. It is the coordinate
system shared by JavaScript strings, VS Code, and the Language Server Protocol.
For text containing characters represented by two UTF-16 code units, a visual
column and a protocol column may differ. Naming the coordinate system prevents
that difference from becoming an accidental off-by-one error.

## Stable identities are independent of presentation

A contribution needs the same logical identity whether it is rendered in HTML,
linked from an index, or addressed from LaTeX. Literate derives stable targets from
source identity and encounter order rather than from renderer-generated markup.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Source[Source path and contribution] --> Identity[Stable logical target]
    Identity --> HTML[HTML destination]
    Identity --> Preview[Preview navigation]
    Identity --> LaTeX[LaTeX label]
```

Each renderer escapes or encodes that identity for its own medium. The shared model
does not contain an HTML URL or a LaTeX command.

## Parsing and generation answer different questions

Parsing asks:

> What documents, contributions, references, annotations, and logical identities
> does this snapshot contain?

Generation asks:

> Is the whole project valid under output policy, and if so, what complete files
> should exist?

A missing template, an output collision, or an invalid theme can prevent generation
without changing the fact that a contribution was parsed at a particular source
location. Keeping these questions separate makes inspection useful and prevents
publication or editor tools from depending on filesystem mutation.

## Pure does not mean trivial

The parser still enforces important boundaries: safe relative paths, declaration
grammar, duplicate source identities, normalized order, source-text validity, and
stable annotations. “Pure” means that its result depends only on explicit inputs
and that it performs no external effects. It does not mean that validation is weak.

The next chapter adds generation policy to this parsed structure. It shows why
Literate constructs the complete output plan before returning even one file.
